    <h2 id="#choosing-a-distribution">Choosing a Distribution</h2>
    <p>A Linux distribution (or “distro”) is a version of Linux that includes the core operating system and additional software. To test a distribution you should:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Spend time navigating the desktop environment. Familiarize yourself with the menu, taskbar, and workspace switcher. Explore default applications like the file manager, text editor, and web browser to understand basic functionality.</li>
        <li>Learn about the distribution. Who runs it? What's the package manager? How often does it update?</li>
        <li>Find out about the distribution's community. Browse through its forums or its Discord server or its subreddit or whatever you may prefer. Ask people who use it what their day to day is like and why they love their distribution.</li>
        <li>See what Desktop Environments it has available for use and if it customizes them in any way.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For more information on the subject, check out the <a href="./distro-list">List of GNU/Linux Distributions</a> article.</p>
